## A render-free core

The surface rests on the simulation being steppable. Two properties make it
so.

- A fixed timestep, decoupled from rendering. The simulation advances only in
  whole ticks of `TICK_DT` (`1 / 60`) seconds, `TICK_HZ` (`60`) of them per
  second of game time. On `playing`, each frame's delta time joins the
  accumulator, every whole `TICK_DT` in it is consumed as a tick, and the
  remainder waits for the next frame. A tick is consumed while the accumulator
  is at least `TICK_DT − TICK_EPSILON`, with `TICK_EPSILON` (`1e-9`) seconds,
  and a remainder whose magnitude is below `TICK_EPSILON` is `0`, so a frame of
  `0.5` seconds runs exactly `30` ticks and sixty frames of `1 / 60` seconds
  run exactly `60`. A tick that leaves `playing`, by opening
  an overlay or ending the run, is the last tick its frame runs, and the
  remainder is discarded; so is the remainder on any other frame or pose that
  leaves `playing`. On every other screen a frame ticks nothing and the
  accumulator holds `0`. An interval of game time on `playing` therefore runs
  the same ticks however it was divided into frames, and drawing advances
  nothing.
- A render-free core. Game state advances from ticks and input alone,
  independent of the canvas, of the frame loop that measured the delta time,
  and of wall-clock time. The simulation reads nothing from the renderer, and
  every hit, kill, drop, collection, level-up, and ending is decided from the
  state alone.

`simTime` rises by every frame's delta time on every screen, and `muted`
mirrors the runtime.

Audio belongs to the ticks and the frames. A pose changes the state alone and
sounds nothing; the cues a scenario hears come from the ticks and frames run
after it. The two looping cues are reconciled from the state by the next
frame, so a run posed through `setScreen("playing")` sounds exactly as one
started from the menu one frame later.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the game, and
the surface carries no operation for either. That layer delivers the key
bindings `specs/controls.md` fixes, so a dispatched keyboard event moves the
lamplighter and works the menus exactly as a player's key does, and it draws
and toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep`, `step`, and `advance` below.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The clock, the keyboard, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the
game frame by frame, and a clock that supplies its own deltas takes it off real
time: a scenario pairs a `ConstantClock` of `1000 / 60` milliseconds with
`engine.advance`, so one frame on `playing` consumes exactly one tick, and a
clock of any other length poses a partial frame. `simTime` and `accumulator`
behave under such a clock exactly as under the wall clock. The engine drives
the actions the game registers, so a keyboard event dispatched at the engine's
event target moves the lamplighter and works the menus exactly as a player's
key does, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the
game frame by frame, and a clock that supplies its own deltas takes it off real
time: a scenario pairs a `ConstantClock` of `1000 / 60` milliseconds with
